\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Left: tokenized LMs suffer from tokenization bias when a prompt ends mid-token, as in this code completion example: the correct continuation \texttt  {a} receives zero probability under next-token prediction. Our method uses the same model but predicts the correct next byte. Right: our method maps the next-token predictions of arbitrary tokenized LMs to statistically equivalent next-byte predictions (Section~\ref {sec:method_repr}), which places all models in a common output space and enables any ensembling strategy, such as averaging or mixture of experts.}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:teaser}{{1}{2}{Left: tokenized LMs suffer from tokenization bias when a prompt ends mid-token, as in this code completion example: the correct continuation \str {a} receives zero probability under next-token prediction. Our method uses the same model but predicts the correct next byte. Right: our method maps the next-token predictions of arbitrary tokenized LMs to statistically equivalent next-byte predictions (Section~\ref {sec:method_repr}), which places all models in a common output space and enables any ensembling strategy, such as averaging or mixture of experts}{figure.1}{}}
